{"step_index":46,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:42:56Z","content":"--- **1 Neck Disability Index NDI** • **Definition and Overview** • Developed by Howard Vernon and Silvano Mior in 1991 as a modification of the Oswestry Low Back Pain Disability Questionnaire • It is a patient reported condition specific outcome measure designed to quantify self rated disability and functional limitation in patients experiencing acute subacute or chronic neck pain including cervical spondylosis radiculopathy and whiplash associated disorders • **Structure and Clinical Domains** • Comprises 10 itemized sections evaluating physical daily activities and symptom impact • Pain Intensity severity of baseline cervical aching/pain • Personal Care washing dressing grooming without exacerbating symptoms • Lifting handling light medium or heavy objects • Reading sustained cervical flexion/postural tolerance • Headaches frequency and duration of cervicogenic headaches • Concentration ability to focus on cognitive tasks • Work ability to perform occupational duties • Driving head turning blind spot checks postural endurance • Sleeping sleep disruption due to neck symptoms • Recreation participation in hobbies sports and leisure • **Scoring Methodology and Formula** • Each of the 10 sections is rated on a 6 point ordinal scale ranging from 0 no limitation/no pain to 5 complete limitation/worst pain • Maximum possible raw score equals 50 when all 10 sections are completed • If all 10 sections are answered • Handling missing sections eg patient does not drive • Minimum completed sections required for validity 9 out of 10 • **Score Interpretation** • 0 to 4 points 0 percent to 8 percent No disability • 5 to 14 points 10 percent to 28 percent Mild functional disability • 15 to 24 points 30 percent to 48 percent Moderate functional disability • 25 to 34 points 50 percent to 68 percent Severe functional disability • 35 to 50 points 70 percent to 100 percent Complete functional disability / bedbound • **Psychometric Properties** • Test retest reliability High with an Intraclass Correlation Coefficient ICC between 0 point 73 and 0 point 98 • Construct validity Strong positive correlation with the Visual Analogue Scale VAS McGill Pain Questionnaire and Short Form 36 SF 36 • Minimal Clinically Important Difference MCID 5 points or 10 percent change in non surgical cohorts up to 7 to 8 point 5 points in post surgical cervical spine patients • **Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice** • Used as an objective baseline on day one of rehabilitation to establish functional limitations • Guides goal setting eg progressing a patient from moderate to mild disability • Provides documentation required by clinical insurance audits to substantiate continued manual therapy cervical stabilization exercises or postural re education • **Clinical Limitations** • Possesses a ceiling effect in elite athletes and high demand physical workers who experience pain during high velocity activities not captured by basic ADL questions • Section 8 Driving is frequently omitted by non drivers requiring re calculation of the maximum denominator --- **2 Shoulder Pain and Disability Index SPADI** • **Definition and Overview** • Developed by Kathryn Roach and colleagues in 1991 • A shoulder specific self administered questionnaire designed to evaluate pain severity and functional disability caused by musculoskeletal pathologies of the shoulder complex eg rotator cuff tendinopathy subacromial impingement syndrome adhesive capsulitis and glenohumeral osteoarthritis • **Structure and Components** • Consists of 13 total items grouped into 2 distinct subscales • Pain Subscale 5 items • How severe is your pain at its worst • How severe is your pain when lying on the involved side • How severe is your pain when reaching for something on a high shelf • How severe is your pain when touching the back of your neck • How severe is your pain when pushing with the involved arm • Disability Subscale 8 items • Washing your hair • Washing your back • Putting on an undershirt or jumper • Putting on a shirt that buttons down the front • Putting on your pants • Placing an object on a high shelf • Carrying a heavy object of 10 pounds 4 point 5 kg • Removing something from your back pocket • **Scoring Methodology and Calculation** • Scored using either a Visual Analogue Scale VAS 0 to 10 cm or a Numeric Rating Scale NRS 0 to 10 • 0 indicates no pain / no difficulty 10 indicates worst pain imaginable / so difficult that help was required • Subscale calculations • Total SPADI Score • If items are skipped/omitted • **Score Interpretation** • 0 percent Best score indicating completely normal function without shoulder pain • 100 percent Worst score indicating complete functional loss and severe intractable pain • A reduction in percentage indicates clinical functional improvement • **Psychometric Properties** • Internal consistency High Cronbachs alpha typically greater than 0 point 90 for both subscales • Test retest reliability Excellent ICC between 0 point 84 and 0 point 89 • Minimal Clinically Important Difference MCID 8 to 13 points a change of at least 10 points is generally considered clinically meaningful across most shoulder disorders • **Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice** • Allows the therapist to distinguish whether a patients primary impairment is pain dominant acute stage or disability dominant chronic or adhesive stage directly modifying treatment choices eg modalities vs aggressive mobilization • Takes less than 5 minutes for the patient to complete making it ideal for busy outpatient clinics • **Clinical Limitations** • Does not address high level athletic performance throwing mechanics or overhead sports participation • Focuses solely on unilateral symptoms less sensitive if bilateral shoulder dysfunction exists --- **3 Disabilities of the Arm Shoulder and Hand DASH** • **Definition and Overview** • Developed in 1996 jointly by the Institute for Work and Health IWH and the American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons AAOS • A comprehensive 30 item upper extremity specific regional outcome tool designed to measure physical disability and symptoms across any single joint or combination of joints along the entire upper extremity kinetic chain shoulder elbow wrist and hand • **Structure and Domains** • Comprises 30 mandatory core items covering three primary clinical domains • Degree of physical difficulty in performing gross and fine motor tasks 21 items such as opening a tight jar writing turning a key preparing a meal pushing open a heavy door carrying a briefcase washing back changing a lightbulb • Severity of clinical symptoms 5 items assessing pain intensity activity related pain tingling/pins and needles weakness and joint stiffness • Psychosocial and occupational impact 4 items assessing social activities work performance sleep disturbance and emotional impact/confidence • Optional Modules 4 items each • Work Module for workers whose upper limb issues impact heavy or repetitive tasks • Sports / Performing Arts Module for athletes musicians and performers • **Scoring System and Formula** • Each of the 30 items is scored on a 5 point Likert scale • 1 equals No difficulty / No symptom • 2 equals Mild difficulty / Mild symptom • 3 equals Moderate difficulty / Moderate symptom • 4 equals Severe difficulty / Severe symptom • 5 equals Unable to do / Extreme symptom • Rule for missing data At least 27 of the 30 items 90 percent must be completed to compute a valid score • Formula for Core DASH • Where n is the number of completed items • Optional Modules Scoring • Scored independently using an identical mathematical formula to produce a separate score out of 100 • **Score Interpretation** • 0 equals No disability optimal upper limb performance • 100 equals Maximum upper limb disability and severe symptoms • A lower score reflects improved clinical status • **Psychometric Properties** • Test retest reliability Excellent ICC equals 0 point 92 to 0 point 96 across various orthopedic upper extremity conditions • Minimal Detectable Change MDC Approximately 11 points • Minimal Clinically Important Difference MCID 10 to 15 points • **Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice** • Captures kinetic chain dysfunction eg how a wrist fracture alters shoulder kinematics and daily upper limb capacity • Enables standardized comparison between different upper quarter diagnoses under the same rehabilitation program • **Clinical Limitations** • The 30 item core questionnaire can be time consuming for elderly or fatigued patients which led to the creation of the 11 item QuickDASH • Does not discriminate which specific anatomical joint of the arm is the primary pain generator --- **4 Oswestry Disability Index ODI** • **Definition and Overview** • Developed by John OBrien and Jeremy Fairbank in 1980 often referred to as the Oswestry Low Back Pain Questionnaire • Widely accepted as the international gold standard condition specific patient reported outcome measure for determining permanent or functional disability in acute and chronic low back pain LBP lumbar disc herniations spinal canal stenosis and post lumbar fusion surgeries • **Structure and Clinical Domains** • Composed of 10 clinical sections each addressing a critical daily life domain impacted by lumbar pathology • Section 1 Pain Intensity • Section 2 Personal Care washing dressing • Section 3 Lifting • Section 4 Walking • Section 5 Sitting • Section 6 Standing • Section 7 Sleeping • Section 8 Sex Life if applicable/acceptable to patient • Section 9 Social Life • Section 10 Travelling • **Scoring Methodology and Calculation** • Each section contains 6 statements scored from 0 to 5 • 0 indicates the least limitation or no pain • 5 indicates the greatest functional restriction or maximum pain • Maximum possible raw score equals 50 if all 10 sections are completed • Calculation when all sections are answered • Calculation when Section 8 Sex Life is omitted • **Score Interpretation and Clinical Grading** • 0 percent to 20 percent Minimal Disability The patient can cope with most living activities Treatment usually centers on ergonomics core stabilization and general fitness • 21 percent to 40 percent Moderate Disability The patient experiences pain with sitting lifting and standing Conservative management manual therapy electrotherapy active directional preference exercises is indicated • 41 percent to 60 percent Severe Disability Pain remains the main problem activities of daily living are significantly disrupted Requires detailed diagnostic evaluation and structured intervention • 61 percent to 80 percent Crippled Back pain impinges on all aspects of the patients life Intervention requires advanced pain management multidisciplinary care or surgical consultation • 81 percent to 100 percent Bedbound or Symptom Exaggeration These patients are either completely bedridden or exaggerating their symptoms requires screening for non organic signs/Waddell signs • **Psychometric Properties** • Test retest reliability Outstanding r equals 0 point 83 to 0 point 99 • Construct and content validity Highly correlated with objective spinal mobility indices VAS and physical performance tests • Minimal Clinically Important Difference MCID 10 to 12 points or a 30 percent reduction from baseline • **Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice** • Establishes functional baseline and tracks progress over weeks of lumbar core strengthening McKenzie extension or motor control stabilization • Serves as standard legal and disability documentation for return to work evaluations and medicolegal reporting • **Clinical Limitations** • Patients from conservative cultures frequently leave the Sex Life section blank necessitating manual formula adjustments • Shows lower sensitivity in identifying subtle functional changes in high level athletic populations with recurrent low grade back stiffness --- **5 Knee Injury and Osteoarthritis Outcome Score KOOS** • **Definition and Overview** • Developed by Ewa Roos and colleagues in 1998 as an expansion of the WOMAC questionnaire • A self administered instrument specifically developed to evaluate both short term post traumatic knee injuries such as ACL ruptures meniscus tears or chondral injuries in young active people and long term joint damage primary or post traumatic knee osteoarthritis • **Structure and Subscales** • Contains 42 items organized into 5 separately analyzed subscales • Symptoms and Stiffness 7 items swelling mechanical clicking/catching morning joint stiffness • Pain 9 items frequency of pain pain during twisting bending stairs walking • Function in Daily Living / ADL 17 items domestic mobility descending stairs rising from sitting getting in/out of bath shopping • Function in Sport and Recreation / Sport/Rec 5 items squatting running jumping twisting/pivoting on knee kneeling • Knee Related Quality of Life / QoL 4 items lifestyle modification awareness of knee problem emotional concern • **Scoring Methodology and Formula** • Every item is scored on a 5 point Likert scale from 0 to 4 • 0 equals None / Never • 1 equals Mild / Rarely • 2 equals Moderate / Sometimes • 3 equals Severe / Often • 4 equals Extreme / Always • Critical Rule The KOOS does not yield an aggregated composite total score Each of the 5 subscales is calculated and reported independently • Transformation Formula for Each Subscale • Alternatively expressed as • **Score Interpretation** • 0 equals Extreme knee problems / Complete functional impairment • 100 equals No knee problems / Optimal joint performance • Note on directional scaling Unlike NDI SPADI DASH and ODI where higher scores indicate worse disability a higher KOOS score indicates better function and less pain • **Psychometric Properties** • Internal consistency High across all subscales Cronbachs alpha ranges from 0 point 74 to 0 point 93 • Construct validity Sport/Rec and QoL domains are far more responsive than standard generic health scales in young and athletic cohorts • Minimal Clinically Important Difference MCID 8 to 10 points across the individual subscales • **Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice** • Addresses the entire lifespan of a knee injury from an acute ACL reconstruction in an 18 year old athlete through the long term progression toward post traumatic osteoarthritis in their 40s • The Sport/Rec and QoL subscales eliminate the ceiling effect commonly encountered when using generic arthritic scales on young physically active patients • **Clinical Limitations** • Completing 42 individual items can produce cognitive fatigue in elderly non athletic osteoarthritic patients for whom the shorter KOOS 12 or KOOS PS was developed • Cannot be expressed as a single global number making brief chart documentation slightly longer --- **6 Western Ontario and McMaster Universities Osteoarthritis Index WOMAC** • **Definition and Overview** • Developed by Nicholas Bellamy and colleagues in 1982 • A disease specific clinically standardized patient reported outcome measure considered the worldwide gold standard for evaluating hip and knee osteoarthritis degenerative joint disease • **Structure and Clinical Domains** • Consists of 24 items broken down into 3 subscales • Pain Subscale 5 items Assesses pain experienced during • Walking on a flat surface • Going up or down stairs • At night while in bed • Sitting or lying down • Standing upright • Stiffness Subscale 2 items Assesses • Morning joint stiffness upon waking • Stiffness occurring after sitting lying or resting later in the day • Physical Function Subscale 17 items Assesses difficulty during daily functional mobility • Descending stairs • Ascending stairs • Rising from sitting • Standing • Bending to floor / picking up an object • Walking on flat surfaces • Getting in or out of a car • Going shopping • Putting on socks / stockings • Rising from bed • Taking off socks / stockings • Lying in bed • Getting in or out of bath • Sitting • Getting on or off toilet • Heavy domestic duties scrubbing floors yard work • Light domestic duties dusting cooking • **Scoring Methodology and Calculation Formats** • Administered using one of two validated formats • 5 point Likert Scale • None equals 0 • Mild equals 1 • Moderate equals 2 • Severe equals 3 • Extreme equals 4 • Maximum raw subscale totals Pain equals 20 points Stiffness equals 8 points Physical Function equals 68 points Total maximum raw score equals 96 points • Visual Analogue Scale VAS • 100 mm horizontal line per item 0 mm equals no pain/limitation 100 mm equals extreme pain/limitation • Total maximum possible score equals 2400 mm • Percentage / Normalized Transformation • Raw scores can be reported individually per subscale or summed and normalized • **Score Interpretation** • 0 No pain no joint stiffness and fully preserved physical function • High Scores up to 96 on Likert or 100 percent Severe degenerative joint disease advanced structural joint compromise marked disability and severe pain • A reduction in score indicates positive therapeutic response eg following quadriceps strengthening weight management joint mobilization or arthroplasty • **Psychometric Properties** • Reliability High test retest reliability ICC ranges between 0 point 86 and 0 point 95 across hip and knee OA patients • Validity Strong face content and construct validity when correlated with radiographic Kellgren Lawrence grading joint space narrowing and Lequesne Index • Minimal Clinically Important Difference MCID A 9 percent to 12 percent relative change from baseline or a reduction of 10 to 15 mm on the normalized VAS score • **Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice** • Forms the primary functional baseline before initiating geriatric rehabilitation protocols aquatic therapy manual joint distraction or closed chain lower extremity exercises • Widely used as the primary outcome measure in international clinical trials comparing conservative physiotherapy versus total joint arthroplasty TKA / THA • **Clinical Limitations** • Features a pronounced ceiling effect in young active patients it does not evaluate running jumping cutting or athletic tasks • The physical function section contains 17 items that can be repetitive for individuals with mild early stage osteoarthritic changes","thinking":"**Prioritizing Specific Tools**\n\nI'm focusing on the importance of selecting the most appropriate tool for each task. The goal is to maximize efficiency. I am making an active effort to ensure that I always choose the correct approach to each problem or challenge, such as choosing a relevant 'view_file' utility where possible.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Usage**\n\nI'm now prioritizing direct tool usage, avoiding nested commands like `cat` within `bash`. I'm actively applying instruction one, to avoid making errors. I also recall the second critical instruction and have started listing relevant tools before executing anything. My next task involves processing the new file, adhering to the specified formatting rules.\n\n\n**Removing Unwanted Text**\n\nI'm now focused on removing specific text, namely the LaTeX formulas. I've identified the lines to eliminate and I am getting ready to implement the removal. I need to be exact to ensure the rest of the text remains unaffected. My current plan is to use a text processing utility.\n\n\n**Deleting LaTeX Formulas**\n\nI've successfully identified the lines containing LaTeX formulas for removal, focusing on accuracy to preserve the surrounding text. I am currently preparing the exact commands to eliminate the identified text. This includes lines 148, 150, 187, 189 and 251. I'm choosing a line deletion utility to handle this process.\n\n\n**Transforming Formatting Issues**\n\nI'm now changing specific formatting issues to improve readability. I'm focusing on replacing hyphens with spaces, converting ampersands to \"and,\" and percentages to \"percent.\" I'm also addressing \"gr